LE Audio fixes for crDroid
Source snapshot: 2026-10-05.

UPSTREAM SOURCES AND COMPATIBILITY

This distribution contains source-code diffs for crDroid branch 17.0 (Android 17).
Other ROMs are outside the current scope. It is not a ROM image and contains no
codec implementation: it sets one build-time aconfig flag value that already
exists in the Android source tree, corrects the LE Audio configuration matcher of
the Bluetooth stack and appends existing LC3 configurations to the Media scenario
list. No driver or codec source is modified.

The affected build configuration directory and Bluetooth stack files in crDroid
retain their upstream Apache License, Version 2.0, terms and attribution notices,
including those of The Android Open Source Project. The added file is a release
configuration value, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
Retain the actual copyright and attribution notices of each affected source file.
This overview does not replace the upstream per-file notices or license an entire ROM tree.

Reference repositories, commits and public sources (as used by crDroid branch 17.0).
Note that crDroid 17.0 takes build/release directly from AOSP (tag android-17.0.0_r1);
it is not a crDroid fork (the 16.0 edition used LineageOS android_build_release):
  build/release                 c85f4aebe46714235599a9bd6430a0fe30c6e8d5  https://android.googlesource.com/platform/build/release  branch=android-17.0.0_r1
  packages/modules/Bluetooth    f3fb97e5f1ddb23e4674d60b91c4f6a4895d9d6b  https://github.com/crdroidandroid/android_packages_modules_Bluetooth

crDroid 17.0 edition, 2026-10-20: source checks only (strict apply and reverse at the
reference commits in this NOTICE); ROM build and device validation are pending. The crDroid 16.0
edition is kept on branch 16.0 of this repository.
crDroid 17.0 builds release configuration cp2a, which inherits the bp4a flag value.

The series was built into crDroid 16.0 on the OnePlus 13 (dodge, CPH2653) and
verified with a dual-mode receiver that advertises EATT, using LC3 with hardware
offload. No device-specific code is involved. The changes affect the LE Audio
client, Volume Control and LE Audio configuration selection of the Bluetooth
stack, which are part of upstream Android.

